---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const trussCfg = {
  curves: [
    { expr: 'P*x/(4*(1 - x*x/16))', label: 'bending moment (simply supported)', color: '#2563eb', width: 2.4 },
  ],
  x: { min: 0, max: 7.9, label: 'position along the span (m)' },
  y: { min: 0, max: 30, label: 'bending moment (kN·m), P = 10 kN' },
  crossings: false,
  height: 330,
};

const staticsCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · Free-body first', note: 'Isolate the structure, replace the world with forces. Every analysis begins with an honest free-body diagram.' },
    { math: '2 · Equilibrium: three equations', note: 'ΣF = 0 in two directions and ΣM = 0 about any point. Two-dimensional statics has exactly three equations per body.' },
    { math: '3 · Statically determinate or not', note: 'If the three equations find every reaction, the structure is determinate. If not, you need material properties — that is indeterminate analysis.' },
    { math: '4 · Internal forces appear', note: 'Cut the structure and equilibrium still holds: each cut exposes axial force, shear and bending moment that the section must carry.' },
    { math: '5 · Dynamics when things move', note: 'Add inertia (F = ma) and moving loads become dynamic: impact factors, resonance, and the reason soldiers break step on bridges.' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/civil-engineering/foundational/statics-and-dynamics-for-civil-engineers">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Civil engineers guarantee that heavy things stand still — and the guarantee is written in
    statics. Every bridge, tower and dam is an equilibrium problem: loads down, reactions up,
    internal forces distributed so no part is overstressed. Dynamics joins when loads move, as
    traffic, wind and earthquakes always do.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="from free-body diagram to internal forces" widget="equationStep" config={staticsCfg} />

  <h2>The bending moment diagram is the design</h2>
  <p>
    For a beam carrying a central load P over span L, the bending moment peaks at PL/4 mid-span —
    zero at the supports, maximum where the beam works hardest. Designers read this curve to place
    material where the demand is: deeper sections mid-span, extra steel at the bottom. Every load
    case draws its own diagram, and the structure must survive the worst one.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="bending moment under a central point load" widget="plotter" config={trussCfg} />

  <h2>Trusses: equilibrium made visible</h2>
  <p>
    A truss is a skeleton of two-force members — each bar either pulls or pushes, never bends. That
    constraint makes the arithmetic pure statics: joint by joint, two equations and two unknown
    bars. It is why trusses were the engineering of the nineteenth century and remain the cleanest
    way to teach the discipline.
  </p>

  <h2>Why dynamics keeps civil engineers humble</h2>
  <p>
    Structures have natural frequencies, and loads with matching rhythms — footsteps, wind eddies,
    earthquake waves — can pump energy in. The London Millennium Bridge wobbled on opening day in
    2000 because synchronised footfall found its frequency. Static strength is necessary; dynamic
    behaviour is what actually surprises you.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> NCEES FE Civil exam specifications; Hibbeler, <em>Engineering Mechanics: Statics &amp; Dynamics</em>.</p>
</TopicPage>
